::ALG2_U05_L06_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=x+9.
{
=A. f^-1(x)=x-9#Correct. Subtract 9 to undo adding 9.
~B. f^-1(x)=x+9#This repeats the original.
~C. f^-1(x)=9x#This does not undo addition.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/(x+9)#This is reciprocal notation.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=x-7.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=x-7#This repeats the original.
=B. f^-1(x)=x+7#Correct. Add 7 to undo subtracting 7.
~C. f^-1(x)=7x#This does not undo subtraction.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/(x-7)#This is reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=4x.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=4x#This repeats the original.
~B. f^-1(x)=x-4#This undoes addition.
=C. f^-1(x)=x/4#Correct. Divide by 4 to undo multiplying by 4.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/(4x)#This is reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=x/5.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=x/5#This repeats the original.
~B. f^-1(x)=x-5#This undoes addition.
~C. f^-1(x)=1/(x/5)#This is reciprocal.
=D. f^-1(x)=5x#Correct. Multiply by 5 to undo dividing by 5.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=2x+3.
{
=A. f^-1(x)=(x-3)/2#Correct. Undo +3, then undo multiplication by 2.
~B. f^-1(x)=2x-3#This does not reverse operations.
~C. f^-1(x)=(x+3)/2#The sign is wrong.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/(2x+3)#This is reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=5x-10.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=5x+10#This does not reverse operations.
=B. f^-1(x)=(x+10)/5#Correct. Undo -10 by adding 10, then divide by 5.
~C. f^-1(x)=(x-10)/5#The sign is wrong.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/(5x-10)#This is reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=(x+2)/3.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=3x+2#The sign is wrong.
~B. f^-1(x)=(x-2)/3#This does not reverse the operations.
=C. f^-1(x)=3x-2#Correct. Multiply by 3, then subtract 2.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/((x+2)/3)#This is reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=3x-1.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=(x-1)/3#The sign is wrong.
~B. f^-1(x)=3x+1#This does not reverse operations.
~C. f^-1(x)=1/(3x-1)#This is reciprocal.
=D. f^-1(x)=(x+1)/3#Correct. Add 1, then divide by 3.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Which composition verifies f and g are inverses?
{
=A. f(g(x))=x and g(f(x))=x#Correct. Both directions must return x.
~B. f(x)+g(x)=0#This checks additive opposites, not inverses.
~C. f(x)g(x)=1#This checks reciprocals in special cases.
~D. f(x)=g(x)#Inverse functions usually differ.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=x-2, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. x+4#This adds both shifts.
=B. x#Correct. Substitute x-2 into x+2.
~C. x-4#This subtracts both shifts.
~D. 2x#This adds functions.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x/2, what is g(f(x))?
{
~A. 4x#This multiplies instead of composing.
~B. x/4#This uses the wrong order.
=C. x#Correct. Substitute 2x into x/2.
~D. 2#This cancels x incorrectly.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
What graph relationship do inverse functions have?
{
~A. Reflection across x-axis#That changes output signs.
~B. Reflection across y-axis#That changes input signs.
~C. Same graph always#Inverses are not always identical.
=D. Reflection across y=x#Correct. Inverse functions switch x- and y-values.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
What happens to domain and range for inverse functions?
{
=A. They switch roles#Correct. Inputs and outputs reverse.
~B. They disappear#Domain and range still exist.
~C. They are always all real numbers#This ignores restrictions; inverse domain and range depend on the original function.
~D. They stay unrelated#They are connected by reversal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
What does one-to-one mean?
{
~A. Each input has two outputs#That is not a function.
=B. Each output comes from exactly one input#Correct. This allows an inverse function without ambiguity.
~C. Every output is zero#That is unrelated.
~D. The graph is always linear#Nonlinear functions can be one-to-one on a domain.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Why might a quadratic need a restricted domain to have an inverse?
{
~A. Because quadratics never have inverses#They can have inverses on restricted domains.
~B. Because outputs are always negative#Not true for all quadratics.
=C. Otherwise some outputs come from two inputs#Correct. A full quadratic is not one-to-one.
~D. Because inverse means reciprocal#Inverse function does not mean reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
If f(3)=10, what point is on f^-1?
{
~A. (3,10)#That is on f, not necessarily f inverse.
~B. (-3,-10)#Inverse does not negate coordinates.
~C. (1/3,1/10)#Inverse does not mean reciprocal coordinates.
=D. (10,3)#Correct. Inverse functions switch input and output.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
If f^-1(8)=2, what is true about f?
{
=A. f(2)=8#Correct. Inverse notation reverses input and output.
~B. f(8)=2#This reverses the statement.
~C. f(2)=1/8#Inverse is not reciprocal.
~D. f(8)=1/2#Inverse is not reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find inverse of f(x)=x/2+6.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=2x+12#The sign is wrong.
=B. f^-1(x)=2x-12#Correct. Subtract 6, then multiply by 2.
~C. f^-1(x)=x/2-6#This does not reverse operations.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/(x/2+6)#This is reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find inverse of f(x)=7- x.
{
~A. f^-1(x)=x-7#This does not undo 7-x.
~B. f^-1(x)=x+7#This is not correct.
=C. f^-1(x)=7-x#Correct. This function reverses itself.
~D. f^-1(x)=1/(7-x)#This is reciprocal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L06_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Which statement is correct?
{
~A. f^-1(x)=1/f(x) always#That is reciprocal notation, not inverse function notation.
~B. f^-1(x) means negative f#Negative f is -f(x).
~C. f^-1(x) means f minus 1#That would be f(x)-1.
=D. f^-1(x) means inverse function#Correct. The notation names the function that reverses f.
}

